      "writtenOn": "2023-05-04T21:20:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Let\u0027s add a unit test for this function.\n\nWe\u0027ll want it to be deterministic, so you\u0027ll need to pass the `nonce`, `encryption_salt`, and `enc_key` as arguments (use a pre-generated key).",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 72,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 100,
        "endChar": 1
      },
      "revId": "50d542271fa02a645f15fdc94d18a434b3132772",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a59834b0_a21629de",
        "filename": "common/fpsensor/fpsensor_auth_commands.cc",
        "patchSetId": 153
      },
      "lineNbr": 100,
      "author": {
        "id": 1293547
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-05T12:30:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The reason that I wrap this into a function is because I want to abstract out the nonce, encryption_salt. Passing the `nonce`, `encryption_salt` and `enc_key` will break the abstraction.\n\nThe only thing we should do is check the data would be the same after encrypted+decrypted, and the result of that is still deterministic.\n\nhttp://shortn/_emXipucuh5\n\n\u003e Note that a test can still be deterministic even if it relies on randomness under the hood. For example, your API might generate a random ID for each object that it returns. This is no problem in a unit test, so long as it makes assertions only about properties that are always true (e.g. the ID is always 16 characters long) rather than asserting specific values.",
      "parentUuid": "2043d53d_392ee8b9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 72,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 100,
        "endChar": 1
      },
      "revId": "50d542271fa02a645f15fdc94d18a434b3132772",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
